<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/foundational/plate-tectonics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Earth's shell is cracked into a couple of dozen rigid plates riding on slowly flowing hot
    rock. They spread apart at <strong>mid-ocean ridges</strong>, dive under each other at
    <strong>subduction zones</strong>, and grind past one another at <strong>transform
    faults</strong> — moving 2 to 10 cm a year, about the speed your fingernails grow. Every
    earthquake, volcano range and mountain chain is the receipt of that motion.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from mockery to certainty in fifty years — how a heresy became geology's grand unifier" widget="timeline" config={tectTimeline} />

  <h2>The map explains itself</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Himalayas are a collision in progress.</strong> India rams Eurasia at ~4–5 cm/yr; the crust crumples upward ~5 mm/yr. Everest is still growing.</li>
    <li><strong>The Ring of Fire</strong> around the Pacific hosts about three-quarters of the world's volcanoes and ~90% of its earthquakes — it is simply the longest line of colliding plates.</li>
    <li><strong>San Andreas = slip, not smash.</strong> The Pacific and North American plates slide past each other; the sticking and snapping is California's earthquake problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Oceans are young.</strong> The oldest seafloor is under 200 million years old — it keeps being recycled. Continents, in contrast, preserve rocks over 4 billion years old.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The lesson beyond geography: <em>a theory without a mechanism is a story, and evidence without
    a theory is trivia.</em> Wegener was right and ignored; Hess supplied the machinery; the
    magnetic stripes settled it. That is how science actually moves.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> USGS "This Dynamic Earth"; Vine & Matthews, Nature (1963); Hess (1962).</p>
</TopicPage>
